A field read off a `dyn` asserts to the one struct that declares the field,
since Go's `any` has none. A library payload a handler names -- here the
`InputEvent` behind `@input(e)` -- joins the package's structs too, and it
declares `value`, so `node.value` found two claimants and was emitted bare:
`node.Value` on an `any`, which neither Go target compiles. The program's own
structs are asked first.

A recursion over state keeps `node` a dyn to codegen, as examples/showcase's
TreeView does; over a constant, or spliced, the read folds.

deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `[^)]node\.Value` -- the dyn read asserts to Item before selecting
deny out/go/fyne/model.go `[^)]node\.Value` -- the dyn read asserts to Item before selecting
-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
        fyne
    }
}

struct Item {
    value int = 0
    next dyn = null
}

component show(node dyn = null) node {
    text(value=string(node.value))
    if node.next != null {
        show(node=node.next)
    }
}

window("t") {
    var typed = ""
    var tree = Item{value=3, next=Item{value=4}}
    input(value=typed, @input(e) { typed = e.value })
    show(node=tree)
}
